# 4. DEFAULT QUICK CONNECT — TWO DIGIT CODE

The default joining method MUST use a two-digit code.

Valid codes:

`00` through `99`

The code must be displayed prominently and be easy to remember and communicate.

Example:

```text
╔════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║                TERMLINK                  ║
║          P2P COLLABORATION                ║
╚════════════════════════════════════════════╝

Room created successfully!

ROOM CODE

                  47

Join from another terminal:

    termlink 47

Invite link:

    https://YOUR-DOMAIN/join/47

Status: Waiting for peers...

Connected members: 1
```

The room code should be assigned dynamically.

The room should not require the user to configure a long token manually.

## Joining

Users should be able to join using any of these methods:

```bash
termlink 47
```

```bash
termlink join 47
```

```bash
termlink join https://YOUR-DOMAIN/join/47
```

Allow invitation links to open the TermLink CLI or an optional companion application if the operating system supports it.

## IMPORTANT SECURITY REQUIREMENT

Two-digit codes contain only 100 possibilities.

Therefore:

- The two-digit code is a room discovery identifier, not an authentication credential.
- Never use the code itself as an encryption key.
- Never use it as the sole authentication mechanism.
- Require host approval for new members by default.
- Rate-limit connection attempts.
- Prevent automated room enumeration.
- Apply short room-code lifetimes.
- Handle code collisions safely.
- Show the room name and host identity before the user joins.
- Make it possible to reject unknown peers.
- Do not expose a room to arbitrary users without permission.

If multiple rooms have the same code, handle this safely.

Implement a room-resolution system. If the code is ambiguous, show available room candidates with limited metadata and require the user to select the intended room. Do not connect to a random room.

## Room code lifecycle

- Room code assigned when a room is created.
- Room code becomes inactive when the room closes.
- Rooms can optionally rotate their code.
- Reconnection should be supported for existing authenticated members.
- Host can disable public quick-connect.
- Host can require a password or additional verification.
- Host can generate stronger invitation links.

