# 31. STRICT IMPLEMENTATION RULES

Follow these rules throughout development:

1. Do not build only a frontend.
2. Do not use mock data for actual networking.
3. Do not simulate peer connections.
4. Do not simulate file transfers.
5. Do not pretend remote terminal functionality is working.
6. Do not use placeholder handlers for completed features.
7. Do not claim tests passed without executing them.
8. Do not leave core features as TODOs while claiming completion.
9. Do not hide incomplete functionality.
10. Do not automatically execute commands received from peers.
11. Do not automatically accept incoming files.
12. Do not automatically enable remote shell access.
13. Do not expose private keys.
14. Do not hardcode credentials.
15. Do not hardcode a domain we do not own.
16. Do not use insecure random numbers for security-sensitive tokens.
17. Validate all incoming network messages.
18. Use streaming for large files.
19. Prevent path traversal.
20. Respect platform differences.
21. Make the application usable in Windows, Linux and macOS terminals.
22. Use modular architecture.
23. Maintain dependency compatibility.
24. Provide proper error handling.
25. Do not remove existing working features when adding new features.

If a feature cannot be completed, clearly state:

- What was implemented
- What is incomplete
- Why it is incomplete
- What dependencies are required
- How to continue implementing it

